5, 0.0274810791015625, -0.01873779296875, 0.00852203369140625, -0.0042877197265625, 0.042938232421875, -0.0144195556640625, 0.009979248046875, 0.017120361328125, 0.04180908203125, 0.034881591796875, 0.0004057884216308594, -0.02716064453125, 0.0277557373046875, 0.007549285888671875, -0.0082244873046875, -0.02313232421875, 0.02264404296875, -0.021514892578125, 0.0172119140625, -0.0004839897155761719, 0.0287017822265625, 0.01119232177734375, -0.022125244140625, 0.033294677734375, -0.0122528076171875, -0.01079559326171875, -0.0087432861328125, 0.0211944580078125, -0.0111541748046875, -0.006885528564453125, 0.0280914306640625, -0.0189666748046875, 0.004924774169921875, -0.0166015625, 0.0257110595703125, 0.0022716522216796875, 0.019500732421875, -0.0122222900390625, -0.0338134765625, 0.0027980804443359375, -0.00615692138671875, 0.00983428955078125, -0.0167388916015625, -0.007106781005859375, 0.0160675048828125, 0.013092041015625, 0.01544952392578125, -0.034637451171875, 0.004726409912109375, -0.007785797119140625, 0.0171966552734375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**Introduction**\n\nThis document provides a beginner-friendly introduction to the basic rules and objectives of chess, a classic two-player board game renowned for its strategy and skill. Visual aids are highly recommended for learning; see the resources and diagrams referenced throughout. An example board setup and labeled diagram are included below.\n\n---\n\n**Overview**\n\nThis guide covers:\n1. Chessboard and Piece Setup\n2. Key Terms (Glossary)\n3. Starting the Game and Piece Movement\n4. Special Moves\n5. How to Win or Draw\n6. Illegal Moves\n7. Chess Notation\n8. Conclusion\n\n---\n\n**1. Chessboard and Piece Setup**\n\nChess is played on a square board with 64 squares arranged in an 8\u00d78 grid, alternating light and dark colors. Each player controls sixteen pieces: one king, one queen, two rooks, two bishops, two knights, and eight pawns. The board is set up so that each player has a white (or light) square on their right-hand corner.\n\n**Piece Placement:**\n- The second rank (row) from each player is filled with pawns.\n- The first rank (closest to each player), from left to right from *their own perspective*, is arranged as: rook, knight, bishop, queen, king, bishop, knight, rook.\n- The queen is placed on the square matching her color (white queen on a white square, black queen on a black square).\n- White's pieces occupy the first two ranks (ranks 1 and 2), and Black's occupy the last two ranks (ranks 7 and 8).\n\n*Tip: Visual diagrams are extremely helpful for setup. For a clear illustration, see [Chess.com\u2019s setup guide](https://www.chess.com/lessons/how-to-set-up-the-chessboard). Remember: \"queen on her color\" and \"white square on right.\"*\n\n**Example Board Setup:**\n\n```\n8 | r n b q k b n r\n7 | p p p p p p p p\n6 | . . . . . . . .\n5 | . . . . . . . .\n4 | . . . . . . . .\n3 | . . . . . . . .\n2 | P P P P P P P P\n1 | R N B Q K B N R\n    a b c d e f g h\n```\n(Uppercase = White, lowercase = Black)\n\n**Labeled Board Diagram:**\n\n```\n    a b c d e f g h   \u2190 files (columns)\n  +-----------------+\n8 | r n b q k b n r | 8\n7 | p p p p p p p p | 7\n6 | . . . . . . . . | 6\n5 | . . . . . . . . | 5\n4 | . . . . . . . . | 4\n3 | . . . . . . . . | 3\n2 | P P P P P P P P | 2\n1 | R N B Q K B N R | 1\n  +-----------------+\n    a b c d e f g h   \u2190 files (columns)\n```\n- *Ranks* are numbered 1\u20138 (horizontal rows).\n- *Files* are labeled a\u2013h (vertical columns).\n\n*For a visual overview of the board and piece names, see [lichess.org\u2019s interactive board](https://lichess.org/learn#/).*\n\n---\n\n**2. Key Terms (Glossary)**\n\n- **Rank:** A horizontal row of squares (numbered 1 to 8). See labeled board diagram in Section 1.\n- **File:** A vertical column of squares (labeled a to h). See labeled board diagram in Section 1.\n- **Check:** The king is under immediate threat of capture. See Section 5 for more.\n- **Checkmate:** The king is in check and cannot escape capture; this ends the game. See Section 5.\n- **Draw:** A game that ends without a winner, due to stalemate, insufficient material, repetition, the fifty-move rule, or mutual agreement. See Section 5 for details.\n- **Stalemate:** The player to move has no legal moves and is not in check; this results in a draw. See Section 5.\n- **Capture:** Taking an opponent's piece by moving onto its square.\n- **Illegal Move:** A move not permitted by the rules (e.g., moving into check, moving a piece in an invalid way). See Section 6 for examples and consequences.\n- **Castling:** A special move involving the king and a rook, allowing both to move simultaneously under certain conditions. See Section 4.\n- **En Passant:** A special pawn capture that can occur immediately after an opponent\u2019s pawn moves two squares forward from its starting position. See Section 4.\n- **Promotion:** When a pawn reaches the last rank, it must be replaced with another piece (usually a queen). See Section 4 for details.\n- **Algebraic Notation:** The standard system for recording chess moves using coordinates (e.g., e4, Nf3). See Section 7 for more details.\n\n---\n\n**3. Starting the Game and Piece Movement**\n\n- Set up the board as described above.\n- White always moves first, and players alternate turns.\n- On each turn, a player moves one piece according to its movement rules.\n\nEach type of piece moves in a unique way. Only one piece may occupy a square at any time. Capturing is optional and occurs when a piece moves onto a square occupied by an opponent's piece, removing it from the board.\n\n**Piece Movement:**\n\n| Piece   | How It Moves                                                                 | Example Move(s)         |\n|---------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------|\n| King    | One square in any direction (horizontally, vertically, or diagonally). Cannot move into check. | e1\u2013d1, e1\u2013e2           |\n| Queen   | Any number of squares in any direction (horizontally, vertically, or diagonally). | d1\u2013d8, d1\u2013h5           |\n| Rook    | Any number of squares horizontally or vertically.                             | a1\u2013a8, a1\u2013h1           |\n| Bishop  | Any number of squares diagonally.                                            | c1\u2013h6                   |\n| Knight  | In an L-shape: two squares in one direction, then one square perpendicular. Can jump over other pieces. | b1\u2013c3, g1\u2013f3           |\n| Pawn    | Forward one square (never backward). On its first move, may move forward two squares. Captures diagonally forward one square. | e2\u2013e4, d5xe6           |\n\n**Diagram: Piece Movement Examples**\n\n```\nExample: White's knight on b1 can move to a3 or c3 (L-shape).\nExample: White's bishop on c1 can move to d2, e3, f4, g5, h6 (diagonally).\nExample: White's rook on a1 can move to a2, a3, ..., a8 (vertically) or b1, c1, ..., h1 (horizontally).\n```\n\n*Tip: For diagrams showing how each piece moves, visit [ChessKid\u2019s piece movement guide](https://www.chesskid.com/lessons/pieces).*\n\n---\n\n**4. Special Moves**\n\n- **Castling:** A special move involving the king and a rook. The king moves two squares toward a rook (either kingside or queenside), and the rook moves to the square next to the king, crossing over it. Castling can only occur if:\n    - Neither the king nor the chosen rook has previously moved.\n    - The squares between the king and rook are unoccupied.\n    - The king is not in check, does not move through a square that is attacked, and does not end up in check. The king cannot castle out of, through, or into check.\n    - *Kingside castling (short):* King moves from e1 to g1 (White) or e8 to g8 (Black); rook moves to f1 (White) or f8 (Black).\n    - *Queenside castling (long):* King moves from e1 to c1 (White) or e8 to c8 (Black); rook moves to d1 (White) or d8 (Black).\n\n**Castling Diagram:**\n\n```\nBefore Kingside Castling (White):\n1 | R N B Q K B N R\n2 | P P P P P P P P\n...\nAfter Kingside Castling (White):\n1 | R N B Q . R K .\n2 | P P P P P P P P\nKing moves e1\u2192g1, rook moves h1\u2192f1.\n```\n\n- **En Passant:** A special pawn capture. If an opposing pawn moves two squares forward from its starting position and lands beside your pawn, your pawn may capture it as if it had moved only one square. This capture must be made immediately on the next move, or the opportunity is lost. En passant is only possible right after the opponent's double-step pawn move.\n\n**En Passant Example:**\n```\nWhite pawn on e5, Black pawn moves d7\u2013d5. White can play e5xd6 en passant.\n```\n\n- **Promotion:** When a pawn reaches the farthest rank (the eighth rank for White, first rank for Black), it must be promoted immediately. The player chooses to replace the pawn with a queen, rook, bishop, or knight (but not a king), regardless of how many of each piece are already on the board. Most often, players choose a queen.\n\n*Tip: For visual explanations of special moves, see [Chess"}}